# Products API <%= edit_link %>

Products API allows Skroutz Merchants to update their product inventory (availability, price, and quantity) 
in bulk.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> Products API is designed for inventory updates and is not intended for creating new products.

<%= partial 'partials/toc' %>

## Setup

### Obtaining an API token

In order to be able to use the API, you need to generate an **API token** from within Products settings
page in Merchants Panel (Merchants > Products > Feed Updates).

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> There can only be one active **API key** per shop at a certain time.
> Creating a new API key will expire any previous one.

### Authorization

Pass in a valid **API token** in the `Authorization` header, prefixed with `Bearer `
as in the following curl example.

A specific `Accept` header is also required.

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/products/batch \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8'
</pre>

## Endpoints


### Batch update products

Update multiple products and their variations in a single request. You can update availability (enabled), 
price, and quantity for up to **500 products** per request.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/products/batch
</pre>

Each product must include all required fields as described in the [Product object attributes appendix](#product-object-attributes-appendix).

The update of each product (and its variations) is processed asynchronously. The response returns immediately
with an `id` that identifies the batch request, which you can use to poll the
[batch status endpoint](#batch-status) for the outcome of each product and variation.

<%= render_recording :merchants_products_batch %>

#### Request Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
|-----------|------|----------|-------------|
| `data` | Array | Yes | Array of [product update objects](#product-object) |

#### Response Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `success` | Boolean | Whether the batch request was accepted |
| `id` | String | Identifier of the batch request, used to query the [batch status endpoint](#batch-status) |

##### Example Request

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/products/batch \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{
    "data": [
      {
        "product_id": "ABC123",
        "quantity": 10,
        "enabled": true,
        "price": 2999
      },
      {
        "product_id": "DEF456",
        "quantity": 5,
        "enabled": true,
        "price": 4999,
        "variations": [
          {
            "variation_id": "DEF456-SMALL",
            "quantity": 3,
            "enabled": true,
            "price": 4999
          },
          {
            "variation_id": "DEF456-LARGE",
            "quantity": 2,
            "enabled": true,
            "price": 5499
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }'
</pre>


### Validate batch payload

Validate a batch update payload without actually performing the update. This endpoint performs all 
validation checks that the batch endpoint does, but doesn't modify any data.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/products/batch/validations
</pre>

<%= render_recording :merchants_products_validate_batch_payload %>

#### Request Parameters

The request parameters are identical to the [batch update](#batch-update-products) endpoint.

##### Example

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/products/batch/validations \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{
    "data": [
      {
        "product_id": "ABC123",
        "quantity": 10,
        "enabled": true,
        "price": 2999
      }
    ]
  }'
</pre>


### Batch status

Query the status of a previously submitted [batch update](#batch-update-products), including the outcome
of each product and, where applicable, each of its variations.

<pre class="terminal">
  GET /merchants/products/batch/:id/status
</pre>

`:id` is the batch request `id` returned by the [batch update](#batch-update-products) endpoint. 
An unknown or malformed `id` will result in a `404 Not Found`.

<%= render_recording :merchants_products_batch_status %>

#### Response Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `id` | String | Identifier of the batch request |
| `status` | String | `in_progress` while any product is still pending, `completed` otherwise. The `completed` status, means that every product update has been processed successfully or not. |
| `created_at` | String | When the batch request was submitted |
| `results` | Array | One entry per product in the batch, see below |

##### Result object

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `product_id` | String | The product's identifier, as submitted in the batch request |
| `status` | String | `in_progress`, `success`, `no_change`, or `failure` |
| `message` | String | Present when `status` is `failure`, describes the reason the update failed |
| `executed_at` | String | When the product update was executed, `null` while `in_progress` |
| `variations` | Array | One entry per variation declared for this product, with the same shape as a result object (`variation_id` instead of `product_id`, no nested `variations`) |

##### Example

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X GET https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/products/batch/6a9810dcd4ff840001bf0f32/status \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here'
</pre>

#### Batch Not Found (404 Not Found)

When the `id` doesn't match a batch request for the authenticated shop:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "not_found",
      "messages": [
        "Batch not found."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>


## Error handling

In case of errors, the response will have a representative HTTP status code
in the `4xx` range. The body would contain an array of `errors`.

#### Invalid Format (400 Bad Request)

When the request payload format is invalid:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_format",
      "messages": [
        "Expected an array of product updates."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Missing Required Fields (400 Bad Request)

When required fields are missing:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_format",
      "messages": [
        "Missing required field \'product_id\'",
        "Product ABC123: Missing required field \'quantity\'"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Invalid Field Types (400 Bad Request)

When field types don't match expected types:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_format",
      "messages": [
        "Product ABC123: Field \'price\' has invalid type",
        "Variation DEF456-RED: Field \'enabled\' has invalid type"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Batch Size Exceeded (400 Bad Request)

When the batch contains more than 500 products:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_batch_size",
      "messages": [
        "Batch size exceeds the maximum limit of 500."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Invalid Shop State (422 Unprocessable Entity)

When the shop is in a state that prevents updates:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_shop_state",
      "messages": [
        "Shop is not in a valid state to perform this action."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

Or when the shop's XML is being processed:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "invalid_shop_state",
      "messages": [
        "We are currently scanning your XML. Please try again later."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

